{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-143892",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2y\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =2({y}^{2})\\cdot 2|y| \\\\  & =4{y}^{2}|y|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
